+
+## Revision 1.4.49 — contribution-aware plugin journeys
+
+**Proposed journeys under FR-125 / AC-094; no plugin UI is claimed implemented.** Derive actual actors from current permissions; installer, account connector, project manager, ordinary user, reviewer and operator describe capabilities, not newly granted roles. Obsidian remains one candidate journey among unlike contribution profiles.
+
+1. **Discover and assess.** A user starts from a real task, sees the capability, publisher/provenance, exact candidate version, compatibility, declared effects and support limits, and can compare the existing built-in or manual path. Catalog metadata, descriptions and images are untrusted presentation inputs. An inaccessible catalog, unavailable version or failed read is not an empty successful result.
+2. **Admit the package and connect the account.** The permitted actor sees the exact package and dependency set, authority being requested, destination and data exposure. Package admission, external-account authorization and project enablement remain distinguishable. Show which step is missing and who can take it; an ordinary member can understand a block without receiving install/admin authority. A changed package, dependency, permission request or destination invalidates the affected preview.
+3. **Enable for an explicit scope.** Show the selected workspace/project, account/connection and contribution, actual effective capability and its limits. Existing assignments or another project's grant must not be inferred. Where a selected profile needs no external account or code installation, omit that unnecessary step without hiding its meaningful effects. A method pack still follows existing review and assignment rather than becoming active through plugin enablement.
+4. **Complete the native task.** Entry points retain the correct subject and context. A connector leads to the selected source/native proposal; an executor to its Task/attempt/Run and evidence; an analysis result to attributable findings; a report to its permission-filtered native basis. A progress indicator or plugin success message is not the completed user outcome. Keep authorized manual/no-plugin paths discoverable.
+5. **Recover.** Show authentication required, incompatible, disabled, dependency unavailable, partial, failed and outcome unknown as relevant facts, with the latest observation time. Reconcile uncertain writes through their operation receipt before retry. Preserve input, context and historical evidence; do not offer an unauthorized fallback. Missing telemetry cannot produce an all-clear health label.
+6. **Change or update.** Present the precise current and candidate package/dependency versions, capability/permission differences, affected projects and consequences. Preserve unchanged working configurations until the admitted transition succeeds. An incompatible candidate or missing required assent leaves the prior valid state or a clearly paused affected scope. Do not imply that downgrading package bytes reverses prior data migration or external actions.
+7. **Disable, revoke, remove or purge.** Explain the distinct action, dependency impact, queued/in-flight work, shared connections and retained records before commitment. Recheck the inspected basis at the effect boundary. Preserve unrelated consumers of shared resources. Report separately what stopped, what may already have completed and what is still uncertain; keep native evidence readable to its authorized audience under retention policy. A data purge has its own policy and authority.
+
+The preview and complete-impact steps above govern planned changes. **Urgent containment uses the existing authorized emergency stop/grant path in revision 1.4.47:** fence the authorized affected scope promptly without waiting for complete dependency inventory, telemetry or a healthy catalog. Show known/unknown impact and actual acknowledgements, then reconcile affected work and evidence. This creates no new emergency identity, bypass or permission to stop unrelated work; an unavailable control remains explicitly unconfirmed.
+
+### Actor and next-action mapping
+
+Map these capabilities to actual configured principals before implementation; no row creates a new role or grants permission.
+
+| Actor capability | Legitimate decision / action | When unavailable or blocked |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Package admission authority | Inspect and admit the exact package/dependency scope | Ordinary users can inspect their permitted status and identify the accountable maintainer; they cannot bypass admission |
+| External account / grant owner | Connect, renew or revoke the selected external account within its native authority | Expired access or a departed owner pauses affected use; an authorized reassignment/reconnection establishes a new explicit basis, never a silently borrowed credential |
+| Workspace/project configuration authority | Enable, disable and choose contribution bindings/defaults within that scope | Show the missing prerequisite and authorized next action without implying workspace-wide authority |
+| Ordinary invoker | Select an allowed contribution and complete the native task under current permissions | Preserve task context and explain permitted manual/alternative paths; no silent provider substitution |
+| Native artifact or Skill reviewer | Review the exact native candidate and take its already-authorized action | Package install/enable does not approve or activate that candidate |
+| Historical reader / operator | Inspect native history, evidence and supported recovery under current access | Provide a preserved native representation/export or disclose the admitted limitation; never require unsafe obsolete plugin execution to inspect protected history |
+
+When several permitted contributions serve the same job, disclose the selected provider and any scoped default, bind the operation to that selection, and allow permitted deliberate selection where the contract supports it. Installation order, an agent's preference or a failed provider cannot silently choose another. Changing a default affects eligible future work only; in-flight and historical work retain their actual provider/version. Different account authority or target effects require a new reviewed selection. Unaffected consumers of a shared connection retain their rights when one binding or departing owner's access changes.
+
+**Contrasting design traces.** Before claiming generality, trace a selected source snapshot and a materially different bounded execution or reporting contribution from admission to native result and removal, plus a legitimate no-plugin workflow. These are design exercises, not a choice to build those examples or executed acceptance. Canonical 05 owns the proof inventory and canonical 07 the presentation checks. Reuse the existing consequential-choice review method rather than introducing a plugin-specific approval system.
