+## Revision 1.4.48 — dependency-aware coordination within existing Zyra owners
+
+**Proposed architecture, not current runtime behavior.** Extend the task/plan and proposal/evidence owners identified in revisions 1.4.46–47, retaining their identity, resource, authority and stopping contracts. The application/assurance graph describes intended behavior and links among requirements, policies, code, cases and observations. The work-coordination graph describes which bounded analysis or action can run, which prerequisites it relies on and which checks become stale. These are distinct meanings connected by exact references; a source graph edge, model path or nominated case does not create execution authority or prove a scheduling capability. Use existing Tasks/plans as the durable owner and a derived view where needed; select schema/adapter extensions only after inspecting their writers.
+
+### Inspected reuse and limits
+
+At `784c2aed8620f91e2d5984adb2c33278f56a3456`, `LegacyService` already has a persisted `active_plan`, bounded chunk generation and result joining (`legacy.service.ts:21805–21870`), queued scenario processing, and plan-ID/progress fencing for commitment (`22846–22864`). Missing mapped scenarios have a bounded requeue (`22147–22156`). Paged scenario planning has bounded refill rounds but is not evidence of a universal 100-item lifetime cap. Existing graph capture, model preview and impact readers describe different objects. This selected inspection supports reusing batching, persistence and receipts; it does not demonstrate a generic dependency-aware unit scheduler, immutable unit-attempt lineage or complete shared-resource declarations. Full runtime acceptance remains open.
+
+### Unit, expectation and dependency basis
+
+A logical unit references one native project/task or selected draft item, a stable identity, its bounded purpose, accountable actor/actual executor, authorized inputs, expected output and admissible observations. Pin the plan revision, exact input/Skill/authority/criterion versions, tool/resource capabilities, check and evaluator version, output/evidence references and attempt generation. These are semantic associations for existing owners, not a newly published schema. A reviewed expectation is independent of the output being checked; output-authored assertions and model confidence cannot certify themselves.
+
+Define the check before relying on generated work. Distinguish structural validity, actual execution, obligation-specific correctness, prohibited effects, evidence sufficiency and human judgment. A zero exit status, attached citation or allowed-file diff can establish a narrow fact while missing the intended outcome. Use independently adjudicated good/fault controls where feasible and preserve unreachable assertions, unsupported semantics and unavailable evidence. A check that cannot establish the expectation returns its supported limitation; it must not invent a business oracle or silently weaken the assertion until green. Some semantic choices require an authorized reviewer rather than a program-only predicate.
+
+| Dependency meaning | Evidence and scheduling consequence |
+| --- | --- |
+| Produced value / observed result | Identify the exact versioned input consumed. Dispatch or reassessment follows its actual availability and validity. |
+| Shared mutable state, device or limited resource | Coordinate use through the existing resource/native transaction owner even if neither task consumes the other's output. Declare conflict keys and lifetime scope; verify the adapter can enforce them. Absence of a passed variable is not proof of independence. |
+| Authority, consent, policy or human decision | Revalidate required current authority before the effect and after waits. Task ordering and a model-generated permission edge cannot grant a right or substitute for native assent. |
+| Environment, prerequisite or joint invariant | Pin the relevant fixture/build/state or approved assumption and the observation establishing it. Independently successful units can still violate a combined obligation. |
+| Unknown or incomplete relationship | Mark the affected dependency/impact claim incomplete; inspect a bounded relevant neighborhood, request evidence or constrain the trial. Unknown impact is not no impact, and no universal full-repository traversal is implied. |
+
+A unit is eligible only when its declared required predecessors have admissible outcomes at the exact relied-on revisions, its current authority and permitted resource reservation are valid, and its shared budget remains. Declare which inputs and checks are mandatory and how optional, refused, failed, cancelled, partial and unavailable predecessors affect the join under existing semantics. A settled promise or completed worker is not a passing obligation. Missing required evidence blocks the dependent assurance claim; unrelated supported work may proceed. Enforce shared-resource reservations and fencing through short native transactions or admitted leases; do not hold a database transaction open while awaiting a model or remote tool.
+
+Plan material dependencies before fan-out. Allow an authorized successor when new evidence changes the decomposition, with rationale and versioned supersession of affected work; do not silently mutate the active basis. Detect unsupported cycles/deadlocks and distinguish them from an explicitly bounded correction/wait loop. Enforce total fan-out, iteration, resource and time limits through existing control owners. Do not require every task to have all four article node types or a visual graph.
+
+### Local correction and integrated revalidation
+
+Independent specialist first passes receive authorized scope and sources without prior findings where independence is claimed; subsequent informed reconciliation preserves original observations, dissent and evidence gaps. Structural collation, known-ID joins, hashing and deterministic policy calculations use existing tested code. Semantic merging, similarity decisions, risk ranking or contradictory expectations may need a versioned policy or separately attributed judgment; they do not each have one inherently correct numeric answer.
+
+A correction return binds the exact failed unit/output and check revision, verdict, evidence, causal hypothesis versus observation, permitted repair scope, expected basis, attempt identity and remaining limits. Preserve successful unrelated outputs and the failed originals. If the repair changes a shared input, source authority, environment, check or plan, determine the relevant direct and transitive dependency closure and mark its prior reliance stale. The closure can cross earlier/later units; successful units that do not rely on changed material remain unchanged. Unknown dependencies limit any preservation claim and require a bounded further check. A repair may need a shared-owner change beyond the initially named file; investigate and obtain any required scope/authority decision rather than forcing a knowingly incomplete file-only fix.
+
+After local checks, verify the combined contract and native downstream readback at the actual declared boundary. Local passes do not approve the aggregate, prove target execution or certify a release. Revalidate the exact current inputs at native apply/admission; preserve historical approvals/evidence on their original basis. Reuse native atomic units, expected-revision conflicts and compensating changes. A verdict may inform a human decision in read-only mode; automatic routing or enforcement needs a supported, explicitly authorized policy and compatible writer.
+
+### Retry, human checkpoints and feedback into planning
+
+Choose retry behavior from failure class, permitted effects, evidence of progress and the shared attempt/time/cost budget. Transient availability, malformed generation, faulty assertion, missing oracle, stale authority, resource contention and uncertain committed effect require different recovery. Reconcile an uncertain effect by original operation receipt before any new mutation. On repeated no-progress, contradictory checks, unavailable authority or exhausted limits, stop the affected work and propose a corrected plan or a specific human decision. Retries, descendants, repeated Continue and revised decomposition consume the aggregate admitted lifetime budget as well as their per-call limits. A new plan or lease cannot reset that budget without the separately required authorization. Three retries is not a universal policy, and repeated failure alone does not establish a bad plan. Workers cannot edit their check, budget or allowed effect to obtain a pass.
+
+Place human decisions at the authority boundary actually encountered: unsettled intended behavior, material scope/egress/irreversible effects, exact protected publication and release retain their established owners. Work within an already approved bounded scope needs no ritual per-node confirmation. Reversibility and blast radius are assessed from actual effects and recovery capability; a test file, copy edit or additive migration is not automatically harmless. Neither aggregate confidence nor an article's final-only approval rule overrides project policy.
+
+Distinguish correction of this attempt from learning for future task selection. Preserve observation, independently investigated cause, generalized principle, applicability/counterexample, proposed planning constraint and affected method version. Feed a reviewed constraint into the effective planning method or project context through existing Skills/Guide governance. It may change future scope, dependency checks, reviewer selection or decomposition as well as worker guidance. Acceptance or one failure alone cannot create an immutable universal rule. Keep local exceptions local, permit no-change conclusions and retire/supersede constraints under the same human governance when assumptions change. Evaluate transfer on held-out tasks and preserve original grades.
+
+Measure correct decisions and missed obligations, unnecessary rewrites of unaffected units, stale-result detection, integrated failures after local passes, correct escalation/false interruptions, end-to-end elapsed time including queue/wait, critical-path observations and actual attributed costs under the existing evaluation owner. A faster parallel run is not necessarily better. Compare against the supported current Zyra procedure and disclose partial instrumentation, false blockers and untested dependencies.
+
+## Revision 1.4.49 — plugin contracts at existing ownership boundaries
+
+**Proposed architecture under FR-125; implementation and runtime qualification remain open.** A plugin is a versioned optional contribution, not necessarily a process, an external SaaS connection, a UI bundle or a package installed by an end user. Multiple contribution profiles may share lifecycle obligations without sharing an execution mechanism. Stay within the existing modular application and domain services. First-party compiled extensions, inert content packages, remote adapters and isolated third-party execution are alternatives to evaluate, not four mechanisms selected for implementation.
+
+### Registration, identity and composition
+
+Use stable namespaced plugin and contribution identities, exact artifact version/digest and provenance, compatible host/protocol ranges, declared extension points and required/optional package dependencies. The package catalog describes available capabilities; a separately scoped binding records intended use; current effective grants and observed operation health retain their own owners. These are logical responsibilities, not a mandate for new tables or registries. Discover actual writers/readers before selecting storage or SDK shape.
+
+Each extension point is owned by its existing domain service and exposes the smallest sufficient public contract. Define its allowed multiplicity, namespace, ordering where meaningful, lifecycle and version/deprecation policy. A global plugin ID does not prevent two contributions from registering the same action, tool, route, renderer or singleton executor. Validate the entire proposed contribution set and dependency closure before activation; reject ambiguity atomically or leave the candidate inert with an attributable error. Do not silently shadow a native route/tool, overwrite another provider or depend on loading order. Contract-compatible composition requires evidence; similar method names are not proof.
+
+| Profile | Existing owner and boundary to extend |
+| --- | --- |
+| Knowledge/source | Integration connection/sync and native KB ingestion, versions and retrieval; connector/source namespace plus stable external identity must be qualified before multiple-account support |
+| Execution | FR-058/T-408, Tasks/attempts and native automation/reporter/evidence; package admission is not a target execution grant |
+| Analysis | Existing task/analysis and evidence/proposal owners; classify actual execution and egress, preserve measured/estimated/unavailable status |
+| Method content | T-422/D-14, exact KB version, evaluation, human approval and separate effective assignment; importing a package cannot activate its guidance |
+| Reporting | Authorized native readers and export/evidence owners; derived formatting cannot replace result truth or broaden data access |
+| Notification/workflow or product/UI | The actual affected native service, permission checks and host UI contract; admit only selected effects and extension points, with their own integration proof |
+
+Do not force every profile through a source-ticket schema such as the existing integration pipeline's `RemoteTicket`, or through the executor task/report schema. Reuse a domain's semantic contract where it fits; compose shared authentication, transport, observability and receipts without conflating different outputs. New ownership needs a demonstrated missing responsibility, not the name plugin.
+
+Contribution identity and user selection are separate. Where several allowed contributions serve the same job, record the selected contribution/version and any explicit scoped default through the existing configuration/task owner. Defaults apply to eligible future work; a changed default does not rewrite historical provenance or silently reroute a queued/in-flight attempt. Any fallback must have an admitted contract, compatible effects/current authority and visible attribution. Never substitute a provider merely to turn a failure into apparent success.
+
+### Dependency and lifecycle specialization
+
+Package/capability dependencies describe which contributions can load or remain usable. Work-unit dependencies in revision 1.4.48 describe readiness and relied-on results. Keep the two graphs distinct and connect them with exact references where one invalidates the other. Resolve and retain the selected package/dependency closure, required versus optional edges and host compatibility; reject unsupported cycles or unsatisfied constraints. Installed, enabled and currently permitted are different predicates.
+
+For planned update, disable, revocation or removal, calculate the affected bindings, dependent capabilities and outstanding work using current authorized evidence. Unknown dependency impact must remain unknown. Validate the inspected revision when committing the transition, fence affected queued/late work and retain historical receipts using the existing 1.4.47–48 owners. A required dependency loss pauses/refuses affected capability; an optional dependency follows its declared degraded behavior. Do not revoke shared credentials, erase shared resources or stop unrelated projects merely because one plugin is removed. Select the admitted failure behavior explicitly; a package dependency never grants an implied permission.
+
+Urgent containment retains revision 1.4.47's independently authorized stop/grant control. Do not require a complete dependency preview, available telemetry or healthy catalog before fencing the authorized affected scope. Record known and unknown impact and actual acknowledgements, then reconcile dependencies, uncertain effects and lawful recovery. Existing emergency receipt, audit-gap, operator authority and scope limits apply; this is not a new control path, unauthenticated bypass or permission to stop unrelated work.
+
+Use the existing migration and recovery owners for plugin-owned configuration/data where needed. Distinguish uninstalling code, disabling use, revoking access and purging retained data. State backward/forward compatibility and recovery prerequisites before updating. Restoring earlier package bytes is insufficient when a schema or external effect has changed; require a qualified compatible rollback, forward repair or authorized manual recovery. Preserve approved records and readable historical evidence under their native retention and access rules.
+
